[size=16pt][b]Run 26: every index built during the sync, and a chain identical to Core's[/b][/size]

2026-09-17. Node commits [tt]e2f1e53c[/tt] -> [tt]3a3289b8[/tt], Bitcoin Core v31.1 oracle on the same machine.

[quote][b]CORRECTED 2026-09-17, and INTERNAL -- not published.[/b] The wall-clock
comparison this report drew against Bitcoin Core is [b]withdrawn[/b]. It set run 26's
19h 34m beside a 19h 14m Core baseline and called that "within about 2% while
building five indexes". Those two runs were not on the same storage: the Core
baseline ran on a [b]Samsung Portable SSD T5[/b] (0.40 GiB/s sequential write), run 26
on a [b]Corsair MP600 CORE XT NVMe[/b]. A Core run handicapped by a portable SSD
against a bmc run on NVMe is a comparison of disks, not of implementations.

The original version listed three caveats on that number -- the ten restarts, the
mid-run binary swap, the Core baseline's unrecorded index settings -- and missed the
one that mattered most. [b]There is currently no valid IBD wall-clock comparison
between this node and Core.[/b] A matched pair is scheduled: both on the same device,
both with every index they support, both with their config recorded before launch.

Nothing else in this report is affected. The parity results below are comparisons of
content, not of time, and they stand.[/quote]

[b]The short version.[/b] A full mainnet initial block download by an independent Bitcoin full node written in x86-64 assembly, finishing with a chain identical to Core's block for block and a UTXO set identical to Core's coin for coin -- while building [b]five indexes concurrently with the sync[/b], including two that Core has no equivalent for. When the download ended, nothing started: no post-sync index build, no adoption pass, no supervisor waiting for the initial download to finish. That absence is the result this run was built to demonstrate.

