[hr]
[size=12pt][b]What this does and does not establish[/b][/size]

[b]Does:[/b] that an assembly implementation reaches Core's exact chain and Core's exact UTXO set over the full mainnet history; that five indexes, two of them without a Core counterpart, can be built entirely during the initial download; that the post-sync phase can be removed rather than shortened.

[b]Does not:[/b] any IBD wall-clock comparison with Core. Run 26 was restarted ten times, changed binaries mid-run, and the only Core baseline available ran on slower storage. Nor does it establish steady-state serving performance -- address history on one of the most-used addresses on the chain ([tt]1dice8EM...[/tt], 3.2 M transactions through the Esplora facade) answers a balance in 4.6 s and 100,000 txids in 11.8 s. Acceptable for a page load; unmeasured under concurrency, and worth attention before anyone calls it fast.

[hr]
[size=12pt][b]Reproducing the parity check[/b][/size]

[code]
H=967450
bmc-cli  gettxoutsetinfo muhash $H
bitcoin-cli gettxoutsetinfo muhash $H
[/code]

Pin [tt]$H[/tt] on both sides. Comparing a live tip against a live tip is how this project previously talked itself into a UTXO divergence that did not exist.
